WEBVTT

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:05.015
Mia, this tray contains oxygen,
this one water, and this one air.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.475
All are substances around us,
but are they all elements?

00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:10.900
Oxygen sounds like an element.

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:12.577
Water sounds simple.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.609
Air looks like nothing,
which is not very helpful.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.191
We need particle
evidence, not appearances.

00:00:19.924 --> 00:00:24.227
An element contains only one type of
atom defined by its proton number.

00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:27.712
Oxygen, carbon, and iron are examples.

00:00:28.196 --> 00:00:31.840
Chemical reactions cannot split an
element into simpler substances.

00:00:32.636 --> 00:00:32.719
A

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:33.872
compound has more

00:00:33.874 --> 00:00:36.051
than one type of atom chemically joined

00:00:36.053 --> 00:00:36.530
together?

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:37.848
Yes.

00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:42.911
A compound contains two or more elements
chemically combined in a fixed ratio.

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:46.762
Water has two hydrogen
atoms for each oxygen atom.

00:00:47.085 --> 00:00:48.980
Its formula is H2O.

00:00:49.472 --> 00:00:52.686
Changing the ratio changes what
substance we are describing.

00:00:53.496 --> 00:00:58.076
A mixture is different substances sharing
a tray without becoming one new compound.

00:00:58.903 --> 00:00:59.684
Exactly.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:02.913
Their proportions can vary,
and we can usually separate

00:01:02.918 --> 00:01:04.181
the components physically.

00:01:04.713 --> 00:01:09.985
Air is a mixture of gases, water is a
compound, while pure oxygen is an element.

00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:13.762
Does a mixture mean none of
the atoms anywhere are bonded?

00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:19.196
Important correction: molecules within
a mixture can contain bonded atoms.

00:01:19.556 --> 00:01:22.601
The different substances are
mixed rather than all chemically

00:01:22.607 --> 00:01:24.559
combined into a single compound.

00:01:24.907 --> 00:01:28.991
Air contains oxygen molecules and
nitrogen molecules alongside each other.

00:01:29.905 --> 00:01:34.429
So I should say the substances are mixed,
not that every atom is completely alone.

00:01:35.214 --> 00:01:35.592
Good.

00:01:35.975 --> 00:01:39.354
Our colored balls are symbols
for atom types, not actual

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.181
colors or sizes of atoms.

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:44.738
We will keep that legend visible
whenever the particle models return.

00:01:45.524 --> 00:01:48.782
The picture is a model, not
a microscopic bag of sweets.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.670
No tasting the props either

00:01:51.047 --> 00:01:55.292
Read these two labels: capital
C followed by small O and

00:01:55.295 --> 00:01:57.377
capital C followed by capital O.

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:58.965
Do they mean the same thing?

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:02.158
Cobalt with different handwriting?

00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:05.175
One imaginary teacher
tear for that answer.

00:02:05.603 --> 00:02:07.422
Co is the element cobalt.

00:02:07.745 --> 00:02:11.698
CO represents carbon monoxide,
containing carbon and oxygen.

00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:13.979
An element symbol starts with a capital.

00:02:14.243 --> 00:02:16.839
A second letter, if present, is lowercase.

00:02:17.626 --> 00:02:21.247
Sodium is Na, magnesium
Mg, and chlorine Cl.

00:02:21.536 --> 00:02:25.161
Capital letters are scientific
information, not decoration.

00:02:25.982 --> 00:02:26.534
Correct.

00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:30.400
In a formula, a small subscript
counts atoms of the element

00:02:30.423 --> 00:02:31.753
immediately before it.

00:02:32.114 --> 00:02:34.640
Water has two hydrogens and one oxygen.

00:02:34.993 --> 00:02:37.815
Carbon dioxide has one
carbon and two oxygens.

00:02:38.217 --> 00:02:40.142
No written subscript means one.

00:02:40.914 --> 00:02:44.067
So HO is not an acceptable
shortcut for water.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.375
I have removed a hydrogen
from each molecule.

00:02:48.176 --> 00:02:49.005
Exactly.

00:02:49.372 --> 00:02:53.968
Sodium chloride, NaCl, has a
one-to-one ratio of sodium and

00:02:53.969 --> 00:02:56.273
chloride ions in a repeating structure.

00:02:56.638 --> 00:03:01.037
It is a compound, but it is not made
of separate little NaCl molecules

00:03:01.038 --> 00:03:02.915
like the individual water molecules.

00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:06.325
Not every compound has discrete molecules.

00:03:06.814 --> 00:03:09.280
That stops my drawing
from becoming too simple.

00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:12.918
Now, place a big 2 in front of H2O.

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:16.977
How many hydrogen and oxygen atoms
are represented all together?

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.209
Two waters, so four hydrogen
atoms and two oxygen atoms.

00:03:22.564 --> 00:03:24.801
The big number multiplies
the whole formula.

00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:25.831
Good.

00:03:26.174 --> 00:03:28.489
A molecule is a bonded group of atoms.

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:33.314
Oxygen molecules have two oxygen
atoms, so oxygen remains an element.

00:03:33.692 --> 00:03:37.921
Water molecules contain different
atom types, so water is a compound.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.742
Two atoms does not
automatically mean compound.

00:03:42.095 --> 00:03:44.349
Two different elements
is the important part.

00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:49.421
Would sodium chloride behave like a
mixture of sodium metal and chlorine gas?

00:03:50.155 --> 00:03:54.289
If its ingredients are dangerous, perhaps
the salt shaker should come with a siren.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.936
The compound has its own properties.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.683
Sodium metal reacts vigorously with water.

00:04:00.018 --> 00:04:01.654
Chlorine gas is toxic.

00:04:01.957 --> 00:04:04.498
Sodium chloride is chemically
different from both.

00:04:04.854 --> 00:04:08.790
We use food grade salt in appropriate
amounts, but that does not make

00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:10.832
laboratory chemicals safe to taste.

00:04:11.609 --> 00:04:15.775
So I cannot predict a compound's
properties just by averaging its elements.

00:04:16.581 --> 00:04:17.136
Correct.

00:04:17.331 --> 00:04:20.916
Hydrogen is flammable and oxygen
supports combustion, while

00:04:20.917 --> 00:04:22.647
water behaves quite differently.

00:04:23.123 --> 00:04:26.532
Making or breaking compounds
involves chemical reactions

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.630
with bonds and energy changes.

00:04:28.922 --> 00:04:31.033
It is not simply mixing more thoroughly.

00:04:31.761 --> 00:04:35.161
Can I separate a compound with a
sieve if the holes are fine enough?

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.464
A sieve does not undo chemical bonding.

00:04:38.879 --> 00:04:40.080
Physical methods can separate mixture
components because they exploit
